  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 306 AM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nSatellite shows a plume of monsoonal moisture streaming\nnorth/northeast across the Desert Southwest and Great Basin. It's\nbeen a rather active night for showers and thunderstorms over\nwestern Colorado and Utah, extending southward through New Mexico,\nportions of Arizona, and into the Gulf of California. It's that\nfar southern end in the Gulf of California where convection is\ntaking on some tropical cyclone characteristics, and may very well\nbe the main player how our convection and locally heavy rain\nthreat unfolds Thursday.  \n\nFor today, models are in pretty good agreement that we'll see flat\nridging in between a digging trough down the West Coast and a\ndominating 600 dm high over the southeast U.S.  This means we'll\ntake on a more southerly flow, with warming temperatures and a\nreturn of weak instability. Precipitable water (PW) values will \nstart to increase, but not until late in the day and especially \ntonight as we see greater moisture advection from the south. This \nmeans most showers and storms will likely be confined to the high \ncountry for most of the afternoon, with chances gradually \nincreasing on the adjacent plains through this evening as we \ncontinue to moisten and see mid level theta-e advection. However,  \neven in the I-25 Corridor, there's just a 30-40% chance tonight, \nwith decreasing odds farther east across the plains where the \nairmass will still be under the influence of the flat upper \nridge. Given the increase in PW, a couple of these showers and \nstorms could produce brief heavy rain.\n\nThursday is still tricky, as the disturbance in the Gulf of\nCalifornia lifts northward and into Colorado. Ingredients will be\nfavorable for locally heavy rain, as PW grows to over 200% of \nnormal, and above daily observed maximums (outside of \nclimatology). In fact, tomorrow's PW will be near the daily \nmaximums typically seen in the middle of August. While impressive \nas that is, warm cloud depths are not tremendously deep given lack\nof significant low level moisture, with models now trending to \nabout 3,500-4,000 feet...still modest. In addition, storm motion \nis expected to be quite fast at 25-30 mph on average. Also, the \nmid level cloud deck is likely to impact diurnal heating and \ndestabilization. Finally, and perhaps most importantly, we're \nunsure of timing of that disturbance arriving from the Gulf of \nCalifornia. It appears the best lift arrives toward 18Z, which \nwould be a bit early for more numerous heavy rainers. However, \njust an hour or two slower could make a big difference. Even if \nthe ingredients would all come together, it would likely take \ntraining echoes to cause more significant hydrology concerns \nconsidering storm motions. Right now, that greater threat in our \narea is shaping up for the northern border area and adjacent \nplains where WPC Excessive Rainfall Outlook is into the Slight \nRisk category.\n\nFriday and Saturday are shaping up to be quite active as well.\nWhile PW values drop slightly, they will still be near 150% of\nnormal. Also, we'll have upper level support with potential to be\nin the right entrance region of speed maxes. Those would be\nejecting ahead of the upper level trough lifting slowly east \nacross the northern Great Basin and Northern/Central Rockies. \nInstability may also have a chance to build slightly given a bit \nmore sunshine and cooling aloft. \n\nSunday is now shaping up to be the transition day as there's\nmodest agreement that the trough axis passes by. Then somewhat\ndrier weather is still shaping up for early next week. However,\nthere are trends toward weak troughiness/shortwaves arriving from\nthe west or southwest so we still can't rule out a few storms\naround. There's decent agreement that we'll continue to see cooler\ntemperatures for this period, with highs averaging near or just\nbelow climatology (sinking into the upper 70s for the lower\nelevations). Also, it's that time of year where any showers will\nbe capable of putting down a dusting of snow for our highest\nmountain peaks.",
